# FABLE-SONNET-EMPTY-STRUCT-20260923-132: `f()` only declares when f's name
# is ABSENT. When f already has a typed mutable binding of its own -- here
# a plain int, not a Structure -- the empty-Structure-body rule does not
# apply, and f() falls to the ordinary call machinery: f is not callable,
# so this stays a located refusal, not a silent reinterpretation.
fn: probe () int
    int: f 5
    f()
    return: 0
end: probe

return: probe()
